{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0302"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge literature/modern-lit","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/literature__modern-lit.g1.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",857],["+"," Fallada's Berlin returned once more at the very end of his life: in the autumn of 1946, ill and exhausted, he wrote Every Man Dies Alone (Jeder stirbt für sich allein), based on the Gestapo file of a working-class couple who left anti-Nazi postcards around the city, in just 4 weeks. He died on 5 February 1947, and the novel appeared later that year."],["=",196]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",829],["+"," Falladas Berlin kehrte ganz am Ende seines Lebens noch einmal zurück: Im Herbst 1946 schrieb er, krank und erschöpft, „Jeder stirbt für sich allein“ nach der Gestapo-Akte eines Arbeiterehepaars, das in der Stadt Postkarten gegen das NS-Regime ausgelegt hatte – in knapp 4 Wochen. Er starb am 5. Februar 1947; der Roman erschien noch im selben Jahr."],["=",194]]}]},
